# tools/ca/caravan_ladder_spine_witness.rish -- the Caravan ladder's orchestration spine stands in one place, and the meter that found it is the wall that keeps it there.
#
# Every rung of the Caravan arc runs the whole correspondence in order: `close_the_quarrel` carries a position, hears a
# finding, answers it, takes a matter up again, walls it, calls a person about the wall, spares a reader who is done,
# and books what is owed. Until fold D each rung wrote that order out by hand -- a rung born from the rung beneath it
# copied its neighbor's whole function and inserted one step, so the spine grew about three lines a rung.
#
# The copy meter beside this one counts BYTE-IDENTICAL bodies, and by that measure the spine cost the ladder nothing:
# no two rungs held the same `close_the_quarrel`, because each was the rung below's plus three lines. That claim was
# always honest, and its scan says so at the top in as many words. What it cannot see is a NEAR copy, which is exactly
# what a staircase is -- so a thousand lines of orchestration rode free past a meter reading 47.
#
# Measured on `20260820.204641`, the spine was **106 distinct lines standing on disk 1,003 times across 21 rungs**,
# 897 of them lines the ladder had already written, counted two independent ways that agreed. Fold D moved that number
# on `20260820.212419`: the spine lives in `caravan/ladder_checks.rye` now, one body taking the rung as a `comptime`
# parameter and running the steps that rung declares, so the staircase became a property the harness derives. **The
# carry reads 0, and 21 rungs hold no spine at all.**
#
# So this witness changed jobs without changing what it counts. It used to size a cost; it is the wall that keeps the
# fold folded, its ceiling lowered from 1,100 to 40 -- tight enough to catch the SECOND rung that writes a spine of its
# own rather than the tenth. TAME's seventy-line ratchet, which four rungs had grown past, reads zero: the one spine
# standing is 19 lines, and the four movements it calls are 15, 37, 27, and 59, every one under the bound.
#
# Read `total=19` for what it is. This scan measures the spine CARRIED ACROSS RUNGS, which is the thing fold D removed
# and the thing that could come back. The whole orchestration in the harness -- the entry, its four movements, and the
# tally helper they share -- is 170 lines, and it stands exactly once.
#
# Five claims, each proven on metal:
#   1. The living ladder's spine counts clean under a named ceiling.
#   2. The fold is read off the ladder rather than believed -- one spine, 19 lines, 0 carried, 21 rungs freed.
#   3. The copy meter's own reading stands unmoved at 47 beside it, which is what proves the lift added no
#      byte-identical carry while removing the near carry.
#   4. The counting itself is right, proven on a two-rung corpus whose twelve lines, seven distinct and five carried,
#      are countable by eye in the fixture -- the same proof, over a corpus the fold never touched.
#   5. Every RED path refuses by name -- a corpus with no modules, a corpus whose modules hold no spine at all (the
#      REDS %97 shape, a count that cannot see what it measures), and a spine carry grown past its ceiling.
#
# Kin: tools/ca/caravan_ladder_copy_witness.rish (the byte-identical meter and its three folds).
# Brief: active-designing/date/20260820/20260820-204641_caravan-ladder-the-spine-the-meter-cannot-see.md
# No network, no key, no funds, no device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ca/caravan_ladder_spine_witness.rish

say "caravan-ladder-spine: Language EN -- the orchestration no rung copies forward any longer is counted rather than recalled, so the fold that answered it stays answered."
say "caravan-ladder-spine: Style Radiant -- measurement beats memory; the meter that found a cost a neighbor could not see is the wall that keeps the fold answering it."
say "caravan-ladder-spine: Lens TAME -- safety first: the ceiling is named, the counting is proven by hand on a corpus, the seventy-line ratchet prints rather than gates, and every refusal has a name."

let living = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_scan.sh"]
assert living.ok else "caravan-ladder-spine: the living ladder scan must exit 0 -- ${living.out}"
assert living.out contains "SPINE_OK carried=0 ceiling=40" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the living spine under its named ceiling"
assert living.out contains "SPINE_MODULES 111 holding=1" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want exactly one of the 111 ladder modules holding a spine -- the harness. The denominator moved 110 to 111 when the Region body folded into caravan/region.rye, which holds no spine, so the numerator stood still."
assert living.out contains "SPINE_LINES total=19 distinct=19 carried=0" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want one 19-line spine standing, every line of it distinct and none carried"
assert living.out contains "SPINE_RATCHET over_bound=0 bound=70 longest=ladder_checks:19" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want no spine past the seventy-line bound, the four the fold answered included"

say "caravan-ladder-spine: living ladder -- ${living.out}"

# The second reading, computed beside the first and asserted on its own. The count above asks how many different lines
# the whole ladder holds; the neighbor walk asks of each spine how many of its lines already stand in the spine
# directly beneath it. Two different questions over one extraction, and they agreed at 897 before the fold and agree at
# 0 after -- which is what made the number safe to build a design call on and makes the zero safe to trust now. A
# reading that agreed with the first by construction would prove nothing, so note what would separate them: a line
# dropped by one rung and written again three rungs up counts as carried in the union reading and not in the walk.
assert living.out contains "SPINE_NEIGHBOR carried=0" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the neighbor walk to agree at 0"
say "caravan-ladder-spine: second reading -- no spine line stands in the rung directly beneath, because no rung holds a spine to carry"

# The meter beside this one, read here so the two numbers stand together -- and now so one of them can be seen NOT to
# move. 47 was the byte-identical carry before the lift and it is the byte-identical carry after. That is the honest
# proof the lift removed a cost rather than trading it for a cheaper-looking one: the near carry fell 897 to 0 while
# the number a lift could most easily have inflated stayed exactly where it stood.
let copy = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_copy_scan.sh"]
assert copy.ok else "caravan-ladder-spine: the copy meter must still stand -- ${copy.out}"
assert copy.out contains "copied_lines=47" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the byte-identical meter standing exactly where the lift found it"
say "caravan-ladder-spine: the meter beside it -- byte-identical carry stands at 47, unmoved, while the near carry stands at the zero this meter asserts above"

# The counting proven by hand, over a corpus the fold never touched. The corpus holds two rungs: the foot writes a
# five-line spine, and the rung above writes those same five lines plus two of its own. So twelve lines stand, seven of
# them different, and exactly five are a line already written -- countable by eye in the fixture itself, which is what
# makes this a proof of the counter rather than a second opinion from it. The living reading now says zero, and a
# counter that could only ever say zero would prove nothing; this corpus is what keeps the zero meaningful.
let corpus = run ["sh" "-c" "CARAVAN_LADDER_DIR=tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_corpus CARAVAN_SPINE_FN=walk_the_case CARAVAN_SPINE_CEILING=10 CARAVAN_SPINE_BOUND=6 sh tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_scan.sh"]
assert corpus.ok else "caravan-ladder-spine: the corpus scan must exit 0 -- ${corpus.out}"
assert corpus.out contains "SPINE_MODULES 2 holding=2" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the corpus read as two rungs, both holding a spine"
assert corpus.out contains "SPINE_LINES total=12 distinct=7 carried=5" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want twelve lines, seven distinct, five carried"
assert corpus.out contains "SPINE_NEIGHBOR carried=5" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the neighbor walk agreeing with the hand count too"
assert corpus.out contains "SPINE_RATCHET over_bound=1 bound=6 longest=top:7" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the ratchet naming the one spine past a bound set to bite"

# RED path one -- a corpus holding no rung modules at all.
let empty = run ["sh" "-c" "d=$(mktemp -d); CARAVAN_LADDER_DIR=$d sh tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_scan.sh; s=$?; rmdir $d; exit $s"]
assert (empty.ok == false) else "caravan-ladder-spine: an empty corpus must refuse"
assert empty.out contains "reason=no_modules" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the empty corpus refused by name"

# RED path two -- real modules that hold no spine. This is the REDS %97 shape: a scan reading a real corpus and
# answering zero, where the zero is the scan's blindness rather than the tree's virtue. Here the zero refuses instead.
let hollow = run ["sh" "-c" "d=$(mktemp -d); printf 'const std = @import(\"std\");\\n' > $d/hollow.rye; CARAVAN_LADDER_DIR=$d sh tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_scan.sh; s=$?; rm -rf $d; exit $s"]
assert (hollow.ok == false) else "caravan-ladder-spine: a corpus with no spine must refuse"
assert hollow.out contains "reason=no_spine" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the hollow corpus refused by name"

# RED path three -- the ceiling has teeth. The corpus carries five lines; held at four, it refuses, which proves the
# ceiling is a real gate rather than a printed number. The proof runs on the corpus rather than the living ladder,
# because a gate must be provable without breaking the thing it guards.
let ceiling = run ["sh" "-c" "CARAVAN_LADDER_DIR=tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_corpus CARAVAN_SPINE_FN=walk_the_case CARAVAN_SPINE_CEILING=4 sh tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_scan.sh"]
assert (ceiling.ok == false) else "caravan-ladder-spine: a spine carry past its ceiling must refuse"
assert ceiling.out contains "reason=past_ceiling" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the ceiling breach refused by name"

say "GREEN: caravan-ladder-spine -- the ladder's orchestration spine stands once, 19 lines in the harness with 0 carried anywhere on the ladder, counted two independent ways that agree, the byte-identical meter unmoved at 47 beside it, the seventy-line ratchet reading zero where rungs had grown past it, held under the ceiling the scan printed above so the fold stays folded, proven by hand on a two-rung corpus the fold never touched, and all three RED paths refusing by name."
